nting task boundaries that split requirement groups, but R2.3 paired with R3.x items feels like it might violate that principle. Keeping groups together is cleaner, so I'll stick with the original approach of Task 0 containing R1.5, R2.1, R2.2, R2.3.\n\nNow I'm settling on the final task breakdown: Task 1 gets the first four from group 3, Task 2 combines the remaining group 3 items with the first few from group 4, and Task 3 handles what's left. This feels like the most natural distribution even though Task 3 ends up small.\n\nI'm realizing the requirement descriptions are pretty bare-bones right now—just referencing the SRD specification without elaboration. Since I can't actually read the SRD document itself, I'm limited in how specific I can make these descriptions, but the stitch agent will have access to that information.\n\nLooking at the example format provided, it seems like this level of specificity is actually the standard—just a reference to the SRD requirement is sufficient. I should also reconsider whether to include the requirements.yaml file in the reading materials. The stitch agent won't need weight information since that's only relevant during the measure phase, not implementation.\n\nOne more thing to verify: the commit messages I'm seeing all use the `[stitch]` prefix in their task titles, so I should make sure my output follows that same convention.\n\nNow I need to validate the weight budget for each task to ensure they stay within the constraints before finalizing everything. I'm recognizing the other key features of head—the multi-file behavior with headers, deprecated syntax, error handling for missing or inaccessible files, and version/help flags. Rather than inventing specifics about the SRD that I haven't read, I'll keep my notes general enough to be accurate while letting the stitch agent fill in the details from the actual specification.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens